            ("markdown", "# Python Basics\n**Objectives:** Print values, work with types and collections, control program flow, write functions, handle errors, and create simple classes. Run the code cells in order. Then try the mini practice."),
            ("markdown", "## Output, comments, variables, and types\n`print()` displays a value. A `#` comment explains *why* code exists. Use descriptive `snake_case` variable names. `None` means no value. **Try changing this:** change the name and notice its type stays `str`."),
            ("code", "# Describe one learner.\nstudent_name = 'Asha'\nage = 19  # int\nheight_m = 1.65  # float\nis_enrolled = True  # bool\nmiddle_name = None\nprint(student_name, age, height_m, is_enrolled, middle_name)\nprint([type(value).__name__ for value in (age, height_m, student_name, is_enrolled, middle_name)])"),
            ("markdown", "## Conversion and operators\nConvert text to a number before arithmetic. Compare with `==`, `<`, and `>=`; combine conditions with `and`, `or`, and `not`. `+=` is an assignment operator. **Try changing this:** change the text score and predict the result."),
            ("code", "score = int('8')\nscore += 2\naverage = (score + 6) / 2\nprint(score + 3, score - 3, score * 2, score / 2, score // 3, score % 3, score ** 2)\nprint(average, score >= 10, score == 8)\nprint(score > 5 and is_enrolled, score < 5 or not is_enrolled)"),
