            ("markdown", "## Functions and scope\nParameters receive arguments; `return` gives a result back. Defaults are used when an argument is omitted. `*args` collects positional values and `**kwargs` collects named values. A local variable stays inside its function; a function can read an outer variable. **Try changing this:** supply a different greeting."),
            ("code", "greeting = 'Hello'\ndef greet(name, opening='Hi'):\n    local_message = f'{opening}, {name}'\n    return local_message\ndef add_all(*numbers):\n    return sum(numbers)\ndef describe(**details):\n    return ', '.join(f'{key}={value}' for key, value in details.items())\nprint(greet(student_name), greet(student_name, greeting))\nprint(add_all(2, 3, 4), describe(name=student_name, age=age))\nprint(greeting)  # local_message is not available here.\ndouble = lambda number: number * 2\nprint(double(5))"),
            ("markdown", "## Pythonic tools\nComprehensions build collections concisely. `enumerate()` adds positions and `zip()` pairs sequences. Prefer a regular loop when it reads more clearly. **Try changing this:** add another student and mark."),
